# Portfolio Redesign — Theme & Styling Prompt for Claude Code

Apply the following design system to the existing portfolio website. Do NOT change any content, copy, section structure, or functionality. Only restyle the visual layer — colors, typography, spacing, layouts, effects, and animations.

---

## 1. DESIGN PHILOSOPHY

The aesthetic is **editorial-terminal hybrid** — a warm, cream-based light mode that feels like a Bauhaus design magazine crossed with a hacker's zine. It should feel like it was designed by a software engineer who has genuine taste. Intentional, confident, and a little unexpected.

**Three words to keep in mind at every decision:** Warm. Sharp. Considered.

---

## 2. COLOR SYSTEM

Use CSS custom properties (variables) defined on `:root`. Every color in the project must reference these variables — no hardcoded hex values anywhere except in this definition block.

```css
:root {
  --cream:        #f4efe8;   /* Page background — main surface */
  --cream-dark:   #e8e0d4;   /* Secondary surfaces, card backgrounds, input fields */
  --cream-border: #d6ccbe;   /* Subtle borders, dividers, separators */
  --ink:          #1a1a18;   /* Primary text */
  --ink-muted:    rgba(26, 26, 24, 0.55);  /* Secondary/muted text */
  --ink-faint:    rgba(26, 26, 24, 0.18);  /* Decorative strokes, faint lines */
  --forest:       #1e3d2f;   /* Primary brand color — headings, buttons, nav logo */
  --forest-light: #2d5c45;   /* Forest hover states */
  --sienna:       #c4522a;   /* Accent color — highlights, underlines, tags, active states */
  --sienna-light: #e8855e;   /* Sienna hover / soft accent uses */
  --sage:         #7a9e87;   /* Tertiary / code string color in terminal blocks */
  --white:        #ffffff;
}
```

**Background:** Set `body { background-color: var(--cream); }`. This is a light mode design — do not use dark backgrounds anywhere on the page except inside terminal/code snippet blocks.

**Never use:** Pure `#000000` black, pure `#ffffff` white as background, any shade of purple, blue, or gray as a primary or accent color.

